In this lesson, we'll look at how to compare, order, and work with integers, which are just the whole numbers and their negatives stretching out in both directions from zero.

First, when you compare integers, picture them on a number line. The number further to the right is always greater, so negative seven is less than negative three, which is less than zero, which is less than four. And here's a key idea: a bigger magnitude, meaning further from zero, does not always mean a bigger number. Negative one hundred is actually less than negative one, because it sits further to the left.

Next, let's add integers. You can imagine hopping along the number line, where positive moves you right and negative moves you left. Or you can use a shortcut: when the signs match, add the magnitudes and keep the sign, so negative four plus negative six gives negative ten. When the signs differ, subtract the smaller magnitude from the larger and keep the sign of the bigger number, so negative seven plus four gives negative three.

Finally, subtracting an integer is the same as adding its opposite. So five minus negative two becomes five plus two, which equals seven. A handy warning: subtracting a negative always adds, so watch that carefully.

